Doug Freyburger wrote:
>
> Having a table of what costs how many skills slots I come up with
> some options for a tuned killing machine at XP 30, then several
> alternate orderings to get from the starting point to there.  Then
> as I level up each time I have the option of enhancing a weapons
> class I compare it to the end point.  If it's on that path I do it.
>

I do something similar, but with XP 15 as the baseline.  I also start
my analysis from the spellcasting side and work backwards.  At that
point in the game, going from skilled to expert is quite expensive, and
should be done cautiously.  The difference in damage bonuses is quite
small.

FWIW:

I pre-allocate enough skill spots to get all unrestricted spell schools
to basic, and one to skilled.  For most character classes, this takes
4-6 skill slots.  This leaves 9-11 available for weapons.  That leaves
enough can get one weapon type to expert, my starting weapon to
skilled, and a third to at least basic.  When two-weaponing, the
equation changes a little.

Monks and wizards are an entirely different animal, however.  With
monks I typically advance martial arts to grandmaster, the various
spell schools to basic as I can, and try to keep one spot available for
a god-given artifact weapon.  With wizards, I will advance attack
spells to expert, dagger to basic, keep one spot open for taking an
artifact weapon to basic, and use the rest on spell schools.

The flow of the game usually takes what I want to do and mangles it
beyond recognition. :-)

